===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To become crimson or deep red; to [[blush]].
#* {{RQ:Bulwer-Lytton Night and Morning|chapter=XIII|passage=Eugenie's quick apprehensions seized the foul thought. Her eyes flashed—her cheek '''crimsoned'''.}}
#* '''1885''', {{w|Alfred, Lord Tennyson}}, “The Ring” in ''The Poetical Works of Alfred, Lord Tennyson'', New York and Boston: Thomas Y. Crowell & Co., Volume 2, p. 662,<sup>[https://catalog.hathitrust.org/Record/006663901]</sup>
#*: ''Father.'' Why do you look so gravely at the tower?
#*: ''Miram.'' I never saw it yet so all ablaze
#*: With creepers '''crimsoning''' to the pinnacles,
#* {{RQ:Joyce Ulysses|chapter=13|passage=Gerty MacDowell bent down her head and '''crimsoned''' at the idea of Cissy saying an unladylike thing like that out loud she'd be ashamed of her life to say, flushing a deep rosy red, and Edy Boardman said she was sure the gentleman opposite heard what she said. But not a pin cared Ciss.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To dye with crimson or deep red; to redden.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|III|1|text=Here didst thou fall; and here thy hunters stand,<br>Sign’d in thy spoil, and '''crimson’d''' in thy lethe.}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Sense and Sensibility|28|page=153|text=Her face was '''crimsoned''' over, and she exclaimed, in a voice of the greatest emotion, “Good God! Willoughby, what is the meaning of this? {{...}}”}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1936|author=w:William Faulkner|title=w:Absalom, Absalom!|url=https://archive.org/details/absalomabsalom00faul|chapter=5|page=138|publisher=Modern Library|year_published=1951|location=New York
|passage={{...}} that sheetless bed (that nuptial couch of love and grief) with the pale and bloody corpse in its patched and weathered gray '''crimsoning''' the bare mattress {{...}}}}

